---
import Layout from "../Layout/Layout.astro";
import InLine from "../Components/InLine.astro";
const citations = [
    {
        Inline: "1",
        mla: "https://www.t4tcaregiving.org/"
    },
]
---
<Layout  title="Theological Mandate" Citations={citations}>
    <div class="u-container flex flex-col content-center">
        <div class="flex-auto h-full w-full pt-40 z-2 text-center">
            <h1 class="text-4xl font-black py-5" data-sy-reveal="chars">
                Interview
            </h1>
            <div class="content-center justify-center items-center flex flex-col">
                <p class="w-3/4 font-bold text-lg p-4" data-sy-reveal="lines">
                    Liz is a care coordinator with T4T, she has been working in healthcare for many years as she studied pre-med and worked at a biomedical company.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Additionally, she has much experience with the trans healthcare process, having worked with T4T for 2.5 years.
                    She’s seen firsthand the challenges that trans people face when attempting to get healthcare,
                    and have chosen to do work with t4tcaregiving in order to help them. She helps trans people in person after surgeries,
                    when they may need extra help. She has kindly allowed us to interview her for our project.
                    Her expertise and in-person experience should provide a better perspective to understand the issues that trans people face.
                </p>
                <p class="py-4">
                    T4t caregiving<InLine Citation={citations[0]} /> is a service to help trans people who have undergone a surgery.
                    They are stay at home caregivers who help trans people who need someone to do some basic tasks after a surgery.
                    They help people by doing anything that the person could not do on their own.
                    Things like driving and remembering to take their meds can be hard for someone who just went through a surgery,
                    and likely is on pain medication. T4T helps with all that stuff so that person can just focus on recovering.
                    It’s a group made up entirely of trans people in an attempt to provide adequate care to trans people.
                    A large reason for their group having been created is the inadequate care for trans people.
                    T4t caregiving is a group organized to immediately address an issue which was observed.
                </p>
                <div class="flex flex-col py-4 w-full items-center">
                    <span class="w-3/4 h-1 bg-black/50 rounded-2xl" />
                </div>
                <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 content-center place-content-center py-8">
                    <h1 class="text-xl font-bold ">
                        Biography:
                    </h1>
                    <p class="px-8">
                        Elizabeth Narvaez worked for a biomedical engineering company prior to working for T4T, making synthesizers.
                        Upon realizing that she didn’t like the 9 to 5 structure of work, she quit. She says that it didn’t work with who she is as a person,
                        and that it wasn’t good for her. During her break, she did some volunteering and eventually found out about T4T.
                        Having had some caregiving experience in the past, she emailed T4T and tried out.
                        She has been working at T4T for the last two and a half years. She really enjoys the work, it’s a much better fit for her life.
                    </p>
                    <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 w-7/8 self-center rounded-2xl bg-yellow-200/50 border-2 pb-4">
                        <h1 class="text-xl font-bold pt-4">
                            The Interview:
                        </h1>
                        <div class="collapse collapse-arrow bg-white/25 bg-base-100 border-base-300 border w-7/8 self-center">
                            <input type="checkbox" />
                            <div class="collapse-title font-semibold">How did you get into the healthcare industry?</div>
                            <div class="collapse-content text-sm">
                                <p class="px-8">
                                    In the interview, we asked about her experiences as a caregiver,
                                    as well as the challenges that many trans people face when they attempt to get this sort of care.
                                    To begin with, she described how it is common for trans people to not have good connections with their parents.
                                    This is part of what creates the need for T4t, as there are many trans people who do not have a great support network to pull on for things like surgeries.
                                    They need help with things like “cooking… cleaning… driving…” After a surgery, it’s common that someone may be required to take many different meds as well,
                                    which T4T helps with. The antibiotics that are common make it hard to think, and T4T will help with anyone that is struggling.
                                    Additionally, it’s helpful to simply have a companion for the recovery period. The largest barrier to this care is cost.
                                    Insurance companies don’t tend to consider much trans healthcare to be needed, so they may refuse to cover it.
                                    Liz also says that sometimes the insurance companies will sometimes “cover a procedure, but then there's a lot of other little things that they don't cover.”
                                    Despite the progress that has been made on getting insurance companies to cover trans healthcare,
                                    there are still many ways in which they do not cover the actual care required.
                                </p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="collapse collapse-arrow bg-white/25 bg-base-100 border-base-300 border w-7/8 self-center">
                            <input type="checkbox" />
                            <div class="collapse-title font-semibold">How can we help?</div>
                            <div class="collapse-content text-sm">
                                <p class="px-8">
                                    An issue like this may seem huge,
                                    but Liz says there are some easy ways to support the trans people in your life.
                                    She says the easiest thing to do is just to be there to support the people in your life that are going through these issues.
                                    Trans people often do not have the same support systems that many cis people do, so it’s important to support them.
                                    The next thing that you can do is to be active in local politics.
                                    Voting in local elections for representatives or school boards can help a lot.
                                    Additionally, Liz tells us that we don’t always need to be focused on the big things,
                                    sometimes the best thing to do is work on the “small things”. Focusing on the big things can be overwhelming,
                                    and sometimes the big things are changing so far(like right now) that it’s nearly impossible to keep up with them.
                                    For instance, just recently the U.S. government claimed it wanted to “find and kill” all trans people.
                                    Large issues like the transphobia rampant within the government are not something that everyone is capable of fixing.
                                    Thus, it makes more sense to focus on the individuals who are suffering from the issues caused by that, rather than the people causing the issues.
                                </p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="collapse collapse-arrow bg-white/25 bg-base-100 border-base-300 border w-7/8 self-center">
                            <input type="checkbox" />
                            <div class="collapse-title font-semibold">What's the best case scenario?</div>
                            <div class="collapse-content text-sm">
                                <p class="px-8">
                                    Liz’s belief is that in a perfect world, all people would have bodily autonomy completely.
                                    Including but not limited to the right to transition, with information free and open to all.
                                    There wouldn’t be a lot of bottlenecks to getting something medically that someone wants,
                                    and everyone would trust people to make their own choices. Additionally, there would be more anti-discrimination
                                    legislation making it illegal for people to discriminate against trans people.
                                    This would include the bathroom bills that are being passed in some states, and more resources available for people seeking support.
                                </p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="collapse collapse-arrow bg-white/25 bg-base-100 border-base-300 border w-7/8 self-center">
                            <input type="checkbox" />
                            <div class="collapse-title font-semibold">Where are further resources?</div>
                            <div class="collapse-content text-sm">
                                <p class="px-8">
                                    Finally, we discussed the further resources available to people wishing to know more about this sort of issue.
                                    Oftentimes, as Liz says, it is “easy to link people a facts-based website.” And as important as those are,
                                    Liz wished to highlight something else for further resources: reading a book by a trans person.
                                    Their first hand experience can be very insightful and may do more to change someone’s mind than a fact sheet on the website.
                                </p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 w-7/8 self-center rounded-2xl bg-blue-200/50 border-2">
                        <h1 class="text-xl font-bold pt-4">
                            Reflection:
                        </h1>
                        <p class="px-8">
                            A reflection on this interview would be incomplete without a discussion of the current state of the world,
                            and the difficulty we faced when trying to get an interview with someone to speak about this topic.
                            I think it is telling that only one person of the 10+ that we emailed was even willing to speak with us about this
                            topic(granted that most of them did not reply at all, and thus we don’t know what their thoughts were).
                            Even Liz, who was gracious in allowing us to interview her and had much insight into the lives and challenges of trans people within the United States,
                            was uncomfortable with the recording of our conversation being hosted on this site. The state of the country right now is one where it is
                            often dangerous even to have your face public as a trans person. Though we focused our conversations on the state of healthcare for trans people,
                            it was impossible for the state of the world not to seep in. The fact that so many trans people do not have strong support networks is because of
                            the rampant transphobia in the world, and within past generations which cuts trans people off from their parents.
                            Health insurance companies are less likely to offer coverage to a group which is being made into a public enemy.
                            The treatment of trans people in the media makes it less likely for people to offer to help a trans person, and the blatant and widespread
                            misinformation about trans people does harm to any trans person attempting to get help. It’s very hard for someone not able to spend a large
                            amount of time researching trans people to figure out which pieces of information are “gaping wounds” and which are “pickle cravings.” And any
                            difficulty deciphering what that means is further proof. There simply isn’t a good and reliable resource for information about trans people, and
                            certainly not one that is widespread enough to combat the misinformation.
                        </p>
                        <p class="px-8">
                            Getting a boots on the ground first person perspective on the issues we’ve been discussing was very helpful to our project.
                            Of course we expected to hear about the systemic issues that trans people faced, how doctors and healthcare providers were
                            unprepared to help a trans patient or how health insurance decided not to cover something medically necessary.
                            We did not expect to learn the interpersonal issues that arise from this. That a lot of this stems back to trans people
                            being effectively cut off from the rest of society or from the people that they’re meant to be closest to. I think we both
                            expected for the solution to be some wide sweeping change that affected most if not all doctors, we didn’t expect to hear an
                            answer that seemed so personal. It wasn’t a wide, sweeping change, it was simply helping out the people around you, and knowing
                            that the sweeping change would come from that. Of course that wasn’t to say you couldn’t protest the treatment of trans people,
                            just that sometimes it's okay to not deal with the huge things, and instead decide to focus on the stuff close to home.
                        </p>
                        <p class="px-8">
                            If we could do the interview over again, we’d probably simply do it earlier. We did it towards the end of the project
                            and it meant that we needed to do this whole section while also working on finals and AP tests. Of the actual substance
                            of the interview, we would have prepared a bit better as the interview was a bit stiff on our part(Liz was great).
                            Perhaps simply having scheduled it sooner and prepared a bit more for the interview we would have gotten different or more information,
                            but the information that we have currently is more than enough.
                        </p>
                        <p class="px-8 pb-4">
                            To end this, I’d like to thank Liz for allowing us to interview her. You were an amazing source of information and
                            answered all of our questions with enthusiasm and energy that was inspiring. Thank you for all the information, and
                            for all the work you do to help the trans people being affected by the way things are right now.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>


            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>


<style>
    .anattempt{
        background-image: linear-gradient(
                105deg,
                #0000 0% 40%,
                #FFFFFF 50%,
                #0000 60% 100%
        );
        background-color: lightgrey;
        opacity: .5;
    }
</style>
